So I was playing SlashEm last night with perhaps my most promising
magic using character ever - an Orcish Flame Mage.  I have dodged more
bullets (metaphorically speaking, of course, as I haven't met many
soldiers yet) in this game than in any in quite some time.

I got a fairly lucky start, finding a magic lamp just lying around in
the mines.  Of course, it was only a hunch that it was enchanted, but
a visit to the lighting shop in Minetown (for some reason there's no
Izchak in the version I'm presently using) confirmed that it was
indeed enchanted.  But not blessed, of course.  This presented a
problem, as the minetown altar was the only one I had found and it was
most definately cross aligned.  So what's a chaotic orcish flame mage
to do?

Well, what I ended up doing was watching in shock as my pet hellhound
(which had grown up to be quite powerful) ate the priest with no
difficulty.  While I didn't have a chance to buy protection, the altar
was now open for conversion, plus I got several spellbooks and an
orange robe.  This, of course, made me quite formidable. 
Unfortunately, the lawful god whose altar I converted wasn't
impressed, and summarily sent an Aleax and a Coatl after me. 
Naturally I ran like crazy, and once I opened up some distance I put
on the uncursed ring of teleportation that I had started with and
teleported to safety just as the Aleax was closing.  Locking myself in
a small room, I hunkered down for a bit, then ended up teleporting
again.  To make things more interesting, a Nymph stole my wand of
fire.  Arrgh!  Even though I had intrinsic fire resistence, my stuff
did not.  Deciding discretion was the better part of valor, I fled
deeper into the mines, quickly finding a wand of striking.  Ah hah!

Climbing back up to minetown, I found no sign of the Aleax or the
Coatl, with Flammie (my Hellhound) looking no worse for the wear after
tearing them to pieces.  Needless to say, I was very impressed.  My
wand of striking in hand and Flammie at my heels, I hunted down the
Nymph and blasted her from across the room.

I heard glass shatter.

But the nymph was dead.  Surely a little bad luck was worth getting my
wand of fire back.

But the wand wasn't on the corpse.

Wands of fire were, of course, glass wands in this game.  Arrgh!

With no permanant source of Elbereth (to say nothing of an emergency
flamethrower), I was in a bit of a bind.  Therefore, I hunkered down
and sacrificed until my god granted me the mighty athame Firewall. 
Now *this* was a weapon!  A bit later, a water prayre allowed me to
bless my magic lamp, and Silver Dragon Scale Mail was mine.  Why not
Grey?  Not a clue.  Silver seemed like a good idea at the time.

Heading further downward, I located the luckstone without much
trouble, then killed Ruggo the Gnome king and his hordes.  Or, more
specifically, I hid on an Elbereth square while Flammie killed most of
them.  I also managed to find Schroedinger's Cat for the first time,
which was quite amusing (It was alive but peaceful).

Sitting on the throne allowed me to genocide a race, which I used to
eliminate master mind flayers because I hate them so.

With all the equipiment I had found and a good negative AC, I easily
cleared out Sokoban (using spoilers heavily because I'm lazy). 
Sokoban yielded the Bag of Holding, which would have been nicer if I
hadn't just found one a few levels above.  Oh well.  An amulet of
reflection would have been just as useless.  Onward and downward!

The rat level was quite easy once I pushed a boulder onto the
Polymorph trap that could have made things unpleasant.  The Kobold
level gave me a bit more difficulty, but I managed to perservere with
help from Flammie and because I kept stepping on Polymorph traps and
turning into a Silver Dragon.

Things started getting hairy when I ate a bat corpse to see if they
stun dragons (the do) and promptly found myself being pursued by a
Xorn who knocked 20 of my HP off in one hit.  Yikes!  I tried breating
frost at it several times, but I was stunned so I kept hitting the
wall.  Eventually it slew my dragon form and I returned to orc form. 
After a brief facepalm, I engraved Elbereth and it ran away, right
into the jaws of Flammie, who devoured it happily.

Onward and downward!  Since there were two stairs down, I ended up in
Grund's Stronghold first.  Proceeding cautiously, I was able to clear
out all the monsters on the open portion of the level, but a quick
check of my inventory revealed no means of bridging the moat, so I
didn't even bother to blow the door down and went off with Flammie in
search of a wand of Frost.

DL 15 was fairly unremarkable except for a Frozen Food store, which
was amusing because I've never seen a store (with the exception of the
Black Market) that deep before.  Unfortunately, my luck seemed to be
turning, as Flammie managed to get himself killed attacking a Giant
Mimic in the store.  With my mighty pet dead, I quickly finished off
the mimic and headed even further down.

DL16 had a graveyard, which made for an interesting battle as I was
swarmed by about 20-30 undead nasties and a Horned Devil.  Judicous
use of Elbereth, my blindfold (to see the Vampires), and caution
resulted in me coming out after only having lost (and quickly
regained) 1 level with virtually full HP.

Having found several wands, I resolved to test them by engraving. 
Let's see...  Healing.  That's nice.  Create Horde?  Oops.

Surrounded by a good dozen nasties (including a werepanther and a
two-headed troll), I was relieved that there was Elbereth already
written in the dust.  I proceeded to Engrave it with Firewall, and
they satisfactorally began to recoil in fear.  To make sure I was
safe, I decided to engrave it some more.

What do you want to engrave: ElberethElberethElberethElberethElbereth

As I engraved I was torn to pieces by the horde surrounding me.  Crap!
 To make matters worse, out of all the unpleasant critters around me,
it was the wolverine that did me in.

Shouldn't the Elbereth that was already engraved have protected me? 
Or did I end up somehow degrading it by the act of writing more?  Or
does it seem like I might have accidentally chosen the wrong engraving
tool at some point?  If that was, in fact, something that would make
me vulnerable, I feel dumb for not knowing it - the tactic seemed to
work in the graveyard, though...  Guess I should have used the wand of
teleportation.  Or the scroll.  Or burned Elbereth with my wand of
lightning...

On the plus side, it's an interesting bones file for when Hearse comes
back online...

So tell me.  Was my death stupid or annoying?

